COPY public.courses (id, catalog_name, code, subject, number, title, section, description, credit_min, credit_max, credit_raw, prerequisite_raw, corequisite_raw, pre_or_corequisite_raw) FROM stdin;
1	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ANTH 4620	ANTH	4620	Language, Culture, and Thought	Other Courses	Can you think without language? Can you talk without thinking? This course delves into these questions, combining linguistics, psychology, anthropology, and digital studies. Students begin with an investigation of semiotic relativity, or how language shapes human cognition, debating the differences between human and non-human communication and cognition, including animal communication and AI. Students then investigate the effects of language in particular on thought-including specific syntactic and semantic forms. Students also examine discursive relativity, or the relationship between cognition and language in use, investigating verbal art, pragmatics and political speech, and changes in writing forms-including literacy, the internet, and interactions with AI. Scholars debate the degree to which these technological developments change, and have changed, thought patterns. Students will investigate these debates and write a final research paper.	3	3	(3)	\N	\N	One prior linguistics or cultural anthropology course
2	Undergraduate 2026-2027	CHNS 3203	CHNS	3203	Advanced Chinese Grammar and Conversation	Other Courses	CHNS 3203 is a required course for the Chinese Minor. It is designed for students who have successfully completed Chinese Grammar and Conversation I (CHNS 3201) at UNC Charlotte, or have acquired knowledge and skills equivalent to successful completion of CHNS 3201 elsewhere. This course aims to improve students’ levels of communicative competence in listening, speaking, reading, writing and cultural acquisition. Students will continue to develop and expand their understanding and use of the Chinese language, especially more specialized vocabulary, and complex sentence structures. In addition to improving the students’ fluency in the vernacular style, the course also begins to introduce students to some basic “Literary style” expressions, including vocabulary, idiomatic expressions and stylistics. The course is conducted in Chinese.	3	3	(3)	CHNS 3201 or permission of the department	\N	\N
3	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ETFS 4132	ETFS	4132	Fireground Hydraulics	Other Courses	Exploration of the application of force, weight, velocity and pressure as it relates to the movement of water on the fire ground. Fire loads and the calculation of needed quantities of water are reviewed. Friction loss and flows are calculated for hand hose lines, supply line and master streams. Problems solved will include nozzle reaction forces, theoretical limits of fire pumps, unusual and complex hose layouts including but not limited to wyes, siamese and supply lines.	3	3	(3)	\N	\N	\N
4	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ETFS 4901	ETFS	4901	Fire Safety Research & Data Analysis	Other Courses	Examines the basic principles of research and methodology for analyzing current fire-related research. The course also provides a framework for conducting and evaluating independent research in the following areas: fire dynamics, fire test standards and codes, fire safety, fire modeling, structural fire safety, life-safety, firefighter health and safety, automatic detection and suppression, transportation fire hazards, risk analysis and loss control, fire service applied research and new trends in fire-related research. The course also focuses on data visualization and how to format data into useful information.	3	3	(3)	\N	\N	\N
5	Undergraduate 2026-2027	EXER 1100	EXER	1100	Introduction to Anatomy and Physiology	Other Courses	Provides a foundation of basic anatomy and physiology terms and concepts using evidence-based study skills and learning strategies. The course is intended to support student success in the EXER 2168 /EXER 2169 Anatomy and Physiology sequence.	3	3	(3)	\N	\N	\N
6	Undergraduate 2026-2027	HAHS 1511	HAHS	1511	Local Social Science: Issues of Health and Quality of Life	Other Courses	All Local Theme courses explore the central, unifying question of what it means to be a member of the “local” community in which we live. Through the study of the experiences of different peoples, cultures, and communities in our city, our region, or our nation students will be able to better understand themselves in the context of the complex and diverse society in which we live. This local theme course explores individual and social aspects of health. Analysis of individual health and illness behavior and theory; the social, political, and economic contexts of health and illness; and the broad cultural, ethical, and religious understandings of health and illness. May not be taken for credit and for a grade if credit has been received for LBST 2214 .	3	3	(3)	\N	\N	\N
7	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ITLN 1502	ITLN	1502	Global Arts/Humanities: Italian Culture in the World	Other Courses	This Global Theme course uses the methods and insights of the social sciences to explore the central question of what it means to be a citizen of the world. Through the study of one or more different peoples, societies or cultures across the globe, students will be able to better understand themselves as part of a complex, interconnected world. Students are introduced to critical studies of culture through a broad engagement with the influence and presence of Italian culture throughout the world. Course materials may draw widely from film, media, the arts, literature, fashion, cuisine, and music. Taught in English.	3	3	(3)	\N	\N	\N
8	Undergraduate 2026-2027	MATH 1241L	MATH	1241L	The Academic Success Experience	Other Courses	This corequisite companion course is designed for students enrolled in a foundational gateway course. It provides targeted academic support, a smaller peer learning community, and mentorship to help students strengthen their understanding of course content and develop skills for long-term academic success.	1	1	(1)	\N	MATH 1241	\N
9	Undergraduate 2026-2027	PHYS 2603	PHYS	2603	Practical Seminar: Cleanroom Microlithography	Other Courses	Students will learn how to use specialized cleanroom equipment for producing micro and nano optical materials, including all the steps for performing micro-lithography.	1	1	(1)	\N	\N	\N
10	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 2121	ACCT	2121	Principles of Accounting I	Accounting	Fundamental accounting principles, with emphasis on the use of financial accounting data and analysis of financial statements.	3	3	(3)	MATH 1120, MATH 1241, MATH 1242, STAT 1220, STAT 1221, STAT 1222, or STAT 2122 with grade of C or above	\N	\N
11	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 2122	ACCT	2122	Principles of Accounting II	Accounting	An introduction to managerial accounting with an emphasis on using accounting information to make decisions.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 2121 with grade of C or above.	\N	\N
12	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3300	ACCT	3300	Professional Development for Accountants	Accounting	Focuses on the transition from student to the Accounting Professional. Students explore various career tracks within public accounting and private industry, along with considerations around pursuing the CPA and other certifications. Students learn how to navigate through the pipeline to employment, including enhancing their interviewing and networking skills, as well as finding and securing internship opportunities. Professional workplace conduct is taught along with the importance of strong ethical behavior.	1	1	(1)	\N	\N	ACCT 3311 or ACCT 3323 with a grade of C or better
13	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3311	ACCT	3311	Intermediate Financial Accounting I	Accounting	Analysis of the financial reporting requirements of corporations with emphasis on the conceptual framework and accounting for assets.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 2121, ACCT 2122, ECON 2101, ECON 2102, INFO 2130, MATH 1120 or equivalent, and STAT 1220 or equivalent, with grades of C or above.	\N	\N
14	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3312	ACCT	3312	Intermediate Financial Accounting II	Accounting	A continuation of ACCT 3311 with emphasis on financial reporting for liabilities and stockholders’ equity. Also a number of special topics including the accounting for investments and the statement of cash flows.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 3311 with grade of C or above.	\N	\N
15	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3323	ACCT	3323	Intermediate Accounting I	Accounting	An introduction to the key concepts, processes, and techniques that support the financial reporting process. Topics include: the accounting cycle, the conceptual framework, primary financial statements and disclosure, and the use of present value techniques in accounting measurement.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 2121 with grade of C or above	\N	INFO 2130 with a grade of C or above
16	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3324	ACCT	3324	Intermediate Accounting II	Accounting	A continuation of ACCT 3323 with emphasis on financial reporting of key balance sheet accounts along with their income statements effects.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 3323 with grade of C or above	\N	\N
17	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3325	ACCT	3325	Intermediate Accounting III	Accounting	A continuation of ACCT 3323 and ACCT 3324 with emphasis on financial reporting issues related to special topics, including the accounting for income taxes, pensions, investments, and leases.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 3324 with grade of C or above	\N	\N
18	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3330	ACCT	3330	Managerial Accounting and the Decision Process	Accounting	The concepts associated with internal reporting and business management using accounting data. Techniques in using relevant data for planning, controlling, and decision making are taught. Other topics include: understanding cost behavior, how to accurately assign or allocate costs, developing strong budgeting practices, and learning about responsibility accounting. Excel and Data Analytics are used throughout the course, highlighting the importance of these in the professional world.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 2122 or ACCT 3323 or ACCT 3361 with grade of C or above	\N	\N
19	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3340	ACCT	3340	Accounting Information Systems	Accounting	An introduction to accounting systems, with particular emphasis on business processes, internal controls, and current accounting technology including data analytics.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 2121; ACCT 2122 or ACCT 3323, ECON 2101; ECON 2102; INFO 2130; MATH 1120 or equivalent; and STAT 1220 or equivalent; with grades of C or above.	\N	\N
20	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3350	ACCT	3350	Introduction to Auditing	Accounting	Introduction to auditing and its role in the capital markets. Students will be introduced to the evaluation of risk, controls, designing and executing tests, and assessing audit evidence. Special attention is given to information technology, data analytics, and comparisons of internal and external auditing.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 3312 or ACCT 3324 and ACCT 3340 with grade of C or above.	\N	\N
21	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3361	ACCT	3361	Intermediate Financial Accounting 1	Accounting	An introduction to the key concepts, processes, and techniques that support the financial reporting process. Topics include: the accounting cycle, the conceptual framework, primary financial statements and disclosure, and the use of present value techniques in accounting measurement.	4	4	(4)	ACCT 2121 with a grade of B or better	\N	\N
22	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3362	ACCT	3362	Intermediate Financial Accounting 2	Accounting	An introduction to the key concepts, processes, and techniques that support the financial reporting process. Topics include: property, plant, and equipment, investments, short-term and long-term liabilities, leases, income taxes, shareholders’ equity, and the statement of cash flows.	4	4	(4)	ACCT 3361 with a grade of C or better	\N	\N
23	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3365	ACCT	3365	Accounting Information Systems	Accounting	An introduction to accounting systems, with particular emphasis on accounting transaction processing cycles, internal controls, and analytic tools and methods commonly used in accounting.	4	4	(4)	ACCT 3361 and INFO 2130, both with a grade of C or above	\N	\N
24	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3371	ACCT	3371	Financial Statement Analysis	Accounting	Provides a framework for analyzing a firm’s performance, for understanding a company’s risk, and for valuing equity based on publicly disclosed financial information. The course integrates key concepts from financial reporting (mostly intermediate), corporate finance, economics, and business strategy and applies them to financial decision-making. A key element of the course is to provide a deeper understanding of the numbers presented in the financial statements and the information embedded in the notes, as well as the tools used to analyze them.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 3362 with a grade of C or above	\N	\N
25	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3372	ACCT	3372	Internal Auditing	Accounting	Introduction to the internal audit profession and the internal audit process. Topics include: the definition of internal auditing, The IIA’s International Professional Practices Framework (IPPF), risk, governance and control issues, conducting internal audit engagements, and data analytics in internal audit.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 3362 (or ACCT 3324) and ACCT 3365, all with a grade of C or above	\N	\N
26	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3380	ACCT	3380	Fraud Examination	Accounting	Examines the fraud problem faced by businesses and focuses on fraud prevention and detection. Involves the study of the different types of fraud as well as an examination of the various elements of a fraud investigation.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 3311 with grade of C or above.	\N	\N
27	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3400	ACCT	3400	Accounting Internship	Accounting	Provides a meaningful work experience in the field of accounting. Requires a minimum of 150 hours of supervised employment, 50 hours of work per credit hour. Student is responsible for securing internship and completing internship application and approval process prior to registering for the course. Internship must be approved by instructor. Application process and course registration must be completed prior to beginning internship. All proposals are subject to departmental approval. Students may not earn internship credit for work at a current employer. Some students work full-time during the period of the internship and should plan schedules for Junior and Senior years accordingly. May be used to meet general elective requirements, up to a maximum of three credit hours.	3	3	(3)	\N	\N	\N
161	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ANTH 3154	ANTH	3154	European Prehistory	Anthropology	Prehistory of Europe; Paleolithic, Neolithic, Bronze Age, Iron Age; archaeological methods and theory; ecology and social systems of early European cultures.	3	3	(3)	ANTH 1501 or ANTH 2151 or permission of instructor.	\N	\N
28	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3500	ACCT	3500	Accounting Cooperative Education Experience	Accounting	Enrollment is required for students participating in a cooperative education position during each semester they are working in a position. Participating students pay a course registration fee for transcript notation and receive full-time student status. Assignments must be arranged and approved in advance. The Cooperative Education Program is only open to undergraduate students; graduate level students are encouraged to contact their academic departments to inquire about academic or industrial internship options for credit. For information, contact the University Career Center.	0	0	(0)	\N	\N	\N
29	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 3900	ACCT	3900	Current Developments in Accounting	Accounting	Topics include: internal and external auditing, governmental accounting, income taxes, managerial accounting and accounting theory. A research project is required.	3	3	(3)	\N	\N	\N
30	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 4220	ACCT	4220	Income Tax	Accounting	An introduction to the Federal income tax system with emphasis on concepts and procedures applicable to all types of entities.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 3361 with grade of C or above	\N	\N
31	Undergraduate 2026-2027	ACCT 4225	ACCT	4225	Accounting Data Analytics	Accounting	An introduction to tools and techniques for gaining insight from accounting data. Students will focus primarily on descriptive and diagnostic analytics, the ETL process, and working with data stored in relational databases.	3	3	(3)	ACCT 3365 with a grade of C or above	\N	\N
32	Undergraduate 2026-2027	CHES 1200	CHES	1200	CHESS First-Year Seminar	Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences	This First-Year Seminar course is for CHESS majors only. A seminar-style learning experience focused around a particular theme that is designed to assist with the intellectual and social transition from high school to college by increasing the involvement of students in the intellectual life of the campus; providing an orientation to resources available to students; and promoting problem solving and writing skills.	3	3	(3)	\N	\N	\N
33	Undergraduate 2026-2027	CHES 1210	CHES	1210	FIT for College Success	Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences	This 3-credit, 8-week course supports first-year and transfer students enrolled in CHESS-FIT during their first year at Charlotte. Course topics, activities, and projects have been designed to foster skills for academic success, enable students to discover and connect to the university, and build self-efficacy and resilience. This is a discussion-based, reflection-heavy, people-centered classroom. Students are expected to attend each class period, take notes, read/ analyze, draft/write/revise, reflect, and engage heavily with course materials as well as their peers, peer mentors, and instructor throughout the duration of this course.	3	3	(3)	\N	\N	\N
34	Undergraduate 2026-2027	CHES 3400	CHES	3400	Non-Residential Studies	Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences	Experience outside the University which provides an alternative learning opportunity to broaden understanding of the major and provide an introduction to various careers. All arrangements for non-residential study must be approved in advance and include a written proposal of goals, methods, duration, hours credit, and evaluation procedures. The University Career Center is available to assist students to locate appropriate work experiences. Student projects will be approved, supervised, and evaluated within the student’s major department. Grading by a faculty advisor may be on a Pass/No Credit basis, ordinarily to be taken in the Junior or Senior year. No more than 15 credit hours of non-residential studies may be presented toward a degree. (Cannot be used toward general degree requirements.) Contact major department or University Career Center for information.	1	15	(1 to 15)	\N	\N	\N
35	Undergraduate 2026-2027	CHES 3480	CHES	3480	Citizenship and Service Practicum	Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences	An interdisciplinary, experiential learning course which examines the relationship between citizenship and service to one’s community. Lectures, reading, and seminars explore the historical, ethical, and political foundations of voluntary service for issues such as poverty, homelessness, and social justice. Course meets for two hours of lecture/ discussion per week and requires completion of 40 hours of voluntary service in the community.	3	3	(3)	\N	\N	\N
36	Undergraduate 2026-2027	CHES 3500	CHES	3500	Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences Cooperative Education Experience	Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences	Enrollment in this course is required for College of Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences students involved in professional work experiences offered through either the co-op (part-time work) or the alternating co-op (full-time work) option of the cooperative education program. Participating students pay a course registration fee for transcript notation. Assignments must be arranged and approved in advance. The Cooperative Education Program is only open to undergraduate students; graduate level students are encouraged to contact their academic departments to inquire about academic or industrial internship options for credit. For information, contact the University Career Center.	0	0	(0)	\N	\N	\N
37	Undergraduate 2026-2027	CHES 4800	CHES	4800	LEADS Capstone Project	Humanities & Earth and Social Sciences	The capstone experience for completion of the Undergraduate Certificate in Leadership, Innovation, Diversity, and Technology. Student are expected to demonstrate their professional experience, growth, and development through the LEADS certificate program in an electronic portfolio format.	3	3	(3)	LEAD 2600 or COSC 2600 and LEADS elective courses	\N	\N
