182	course	ANTH 4625	ANTH 4625 Anthropological Approaches to AI Explores the cultural and structural implications of “AI” technology, starting with definitions of what these tools are, are not, and what we are being told they are. Through readings and discussion, we will explore “AI” interventions into health care, government, the creative arts, and education, among other realms. The intent is to acquire a vocabulary for critical engagement with “AI” tools and discourses alike.	{"code": "ANTH 4625", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
183	course	ANTH 4630	ANTH 4630 Spatial Analysis and Anthropology An introduction to Artificial Intelligence, Machine Learning, Geographic Information Systems, Spatial Analysis, and mapping from an explicitly anthropological perspective. Integrating perspectives from Cultural Ecology and Settlement Ecology this course investigates the cultural dimensions of spatial information, while relying on data from Latin America. This course provides a foundation for a future second course offering focused on advanced primary analysis through GIS, such as remote sensing, with intensive AI applications. Students will review numerous spatial theories underlying anthropology and GIS analysis in anthropology, as well as surveying the tools and themes used to collect, describe, model, and analyze data.	{"code": "ANTH 4630", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
