475	course	BIOL 4261	BIOL 4261 Genome Stability and Human Disease DNA damage arises from environmental and endogenous insults, and a variety of specialized cellular mechanisms enable the efficient and accurate repair of lesions. When repair processes are impaired in humans, substantial developmental defects and predisposition to cancer may result. This course introduces students to the principal DNA repair pathways and the diseases induced by inherited repair defects, with an emphasis on interpreting primary research. The aim of this course is to build on students’ basic knowledge of biological principles to provide in-depth knowledge regarding the relationship between DNA repair dysfunction and human genetic diseases, including certain cancers.	{"code": "BIOL 4261", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
476	course	BIOL 4265	BIOL 4265 Drugs: Molecular and Cellular Mechanisms A detailed focus on representative drugs and their target cells and organs to understand mechanisms of action at a molecular and cellular level. Drug discovery, approval, and economics are also discussed.	{"code": "BIOL 4265", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
