487	course	BIOL 4300	BIOL 4300 Life: How We Got Here A broad examination of the evolution of life - from abiogenesis to the evolution of replicators, cells, individuals, and societies, with a primary focus of understanding the major transitions in evolution and biological complexity. Topics relating to the future of life - synthetic biology, AI, and climate change - are also considered.	{"code": "BIOL 4300", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
488	course	BIOL 4405	BIOL 4405 Internship/Laboratory Research A biotechnology-oriented internship with either an organization or within a biotechnology-related laboratory within the Departments of Biological Sciences, Civil and Environmental Engineering, or Chemistry.	{"code": "BIOL 4405", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
489	course	BIOL 4600	BIOL 4600 Senior Seminar Student presentation of oral and written reports from pertinent biological literature. Exit exam for Biology majors is administered.	{"code": "BIOL 4600", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3812	occupation	11-3051.01	Quality Control Systems Managers Plan, direct, or coordinate quality assurance programs. Formulate quality control policies and control quality of laboratory and production efforts.	{"soc_code": "11-3051.01"}	\N
490	course	BIOL 4601	BIOL 4601 Honors Seminar Open by invitation to juniors. Exploration of the nature of science, ethics in science, critical analysis, hypothesis testing and statistical analysis, peer review, and research skills. Students analyze professional research papers, present their analyses orally, select an Honors Advisor, and write a research proposal. Exit exam for biology majors will be administered. Two lecture hours with occasional additional hours to attend special lectures and seminars.	{"code": "BIOL 4601", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
