1807	course	HIST 2145	HIST 2145 Feeding a Mighty Nation: Food Production, Consumption, and Marketing in the USA Capitalism has profoundly shaped food production, marketing and consumption in the USA. Where and how food is grown, what kinds of food, the quality, smell, look, taste and cost of food are all shaped by market forces and influenced by marketers. Large-scale corporate conglomerates and marketing agencies play a significant role in food production and consumption, while organic farming has emerged recently as an alternative source of food. Low-wage workers, however, are the backbone of the food production and distribution chain. They toil in the fields, livestock feedlots, fisheries, processing plants, drive trucks, and work in grocery stores and in fast food industries. This lecture and discussion course in Food Studies surveys the evolution of food production, marketing and consumption from the colonial era to the present.	{"code": "HIST 2145", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
