1907	course	HGHR 3110	HGHR 3110 Resistance During the Holocaust Modern history has given rise to various forms of repression, but no system – not even Nazi Germany – succeeded in extinguishing the desire for greater freedom. The subjects and victims of the Third Reich devised varied, creative ways to resist Nazi tyranny, preserve pre-Nazi political and social traditions, and assert their dignity. Through memoirs and other readings, the range of responses and survival strategies of Jews, dissident Germans, and peoples of Poland, France, and other German-occupied lands are explored.	{"code": "HGHR 3110", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
