1925	course	HCAI 1100	HCAI 1100 AI Fundamentals and Literacy for All Builds essential AI literacy by teaching students to critically evaluate, communicate, and collaborate effectively with AI technologies across personal, academic, and professional contexts. Students will explore fundamental concepts of artificial intelligence, focusing on how to interact with Generative AI tools through responsible use practices. The curriculum emphasizes the analysis of critical socio-technical issues, including privacy, algorithmic bias, data security, and the impact of automation on society. Ultimately, participants will be equipped to critically evaluate the advantages and limitations of applying AI systems within diverse interdisciplinary environments.	{"code": "HCAI 1100", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
1926	course	HCAI 2100	HCAI 2100 Computing, Labor, and Power in History and Society Examines the history and evolution of artificial intelligence through the lens of the humanities and social sciences, moving beyond technical mechanics to investigate the ethical systems that shape computing. Students will trace the progression of “thinking machines” from early machine systems to modern realities like algorithmic bias, analyzing how technology intersects with creativity, power, and society. Building on foundational AI literacy, the curriculum integrates interdisciplinary perspectives to explore how automation transforms labor systems, society, and human communication. Ultimately, the course prepares students to become critically aware thinkers capable of understanding the profound social and political impacts of applied artificial intelligence.	{"code": "HCAI 2100", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
1927	course	HCAI 2101	HCAI 2101 AI Application and Impact Across Disciplines Designed for students across all disciplines, this foundational course provides the critical analysis skills necessary to understand and engage with the future of artificial intelligence. It offers a panoramic overview of AI’s operational principles and innovative applications to demonstrate why this technology matters for every career path. Through discovery and experimentation, learners will explore how AI is reshaping diverse sectors, ranging from medical diagnostics to climate modeling. Crucially, the curriculum emphasizes data ethics and policy, teaching students to identify bias and promote responsible, ethical and literacy-based AI practices in global contexts.	{"code": "HCAI 2101", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
1928	course	HCAI 3111	HCAI 3111 HCAI Social Science Applied Uses social science theoretical lenses, research methodologies, and ethical frameworks to analyze, critique, and assess the usefulness, potential harms, and societal value of AI applied practices in society. This course is cross-listed with social science disciplinary courses offered. These foundational courses, taught by faculty with interests in AI ethics, governance, human-AI interaction, environmental, ecological, and behavioral impacts, and AI pedagogical application move students beyond theoretical frameworks toward more critical and applied AI in Social Sciences. Emphasis will be placed on the foundational skills most needed in contemporary workplaces and applying AI to solve contemporary problems and critically address the major societal and ethical issues posed by artificial intelligence.	{"code": "HCAI 3111", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
