3312	course	SOWK 4230	SOWK 4230 Advocacy and Aging Introduces learners to the impact ageism has on us as we age and the role that advocacy can play in addressing ageism. Learners will develop skills in identifying ageist systems and increase their understanding of the use of social media as a tool for advocacy with practical hands-on experiences. Topics Include: Universal and historical context of ageism & ageist systems of oppression; Intersectionality in aging & advancing equity for older adults; Ethical Responsibilities to Older Adults & the Broader Society; Advocacy efforts for people living with dementia; Framing language when advocating; and Tools and strategies that help to disrupt ageist language, using social media.	{"code": "SOWK 4230", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3313	course	SOWK 4280	SOWK 4280 The Experience of Dementia An overview of Alzheimer’s disease and related dementias with a focus on the biological, neurological, and social aspects of these diseases. Students will gain a holistic insight into these diseases and their implications for both individuals and society.	{"code": "SOWK 4280", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3314	course	SOWK 4353	SOWK 4353 Environments for Aging With roots in environmental psychology, this course explores the ways in which older adults experience and interact with their physical environment at both the macro and micro levels. Students will be introduced to the Americans with Disabilities Act, Universal Design, Age-Friendly communities, and housing options for older adults. This course will examine these programs from a national and local viewpoint.	{"code": "SOWK 4353", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3315	course	SOWK 4365	SOWK 4365 Grief and Loss Across the Lifespan Prepares the professional practitioner to understand and respond effectively to individuals, families, groups, organizations and communities experiencing both symbolic and tangible losses and accompanying grief reactions. Topics include: theories of normal and complicated grief, factors that influence grief at different stages of the life span, cultural and spiritual influences, traumatic loss, anticipatory grief and end of life care, the impact of loss and working in close contact with grief on professionals, and skills and strategies that address therapeutic needs of vulnerable and resilient populations experiencing grief.	{"code": "SOWK 4365", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3316	course	SOWK 4366	SOWK 4366 Early Childhood Mental Health Highlights the relevance of early relationships between children and their parents, families, and other care providers to later health, mental health, and social adjustment. The emphasis will be on how the social environment and neuroanatomy during the earliest years of life interact to shape later functioning. This course will cover basic evidence-based prevention and interventions for young children and their caregivers. Students should have some familiarity with child developmental theory.	{"code": "SOWK 4366", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3317	course	SOWK 4367	SOWK 4367 Child Centered Social Work An in-depth exploration of child-centered social work practice, emphasizing both theoretical foundations and practical applications. It provides a comprehensive overview of methodologies and approaches aimed at supporting the well-being and development of children in various social contexts. Students will explore an ecological-developmental framework for helping children. The course will cover a range of intervention strategies, including family engagement, play therapy, group work, and school-based interventions. Key topics include assessing child welfare, promoting resilience, and advocating for children’s rights within systems such as education and child welfare. Through case studies, role-playing exercises, and discussions, students will develop skills in communication, assessment, and intervention tailored to the unique needs of children.	{"code": "SOWK 4367", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3318	course	SOWK 4368	SOWK 4368 Racism in Social Work: Dismantling Racism in America The purpose of this course is to prepare students to respond to social work’s professional obligations to counter discrimination and oppression and to practice effectively with marginalized groups and those from differing cultures and life experiences. The course assumes that competent social work practice requires an understanding of dominant and non-dominant cultural structures and client differences. It underscores the personal and professional use of self within various cultural contexts and among varying client populations. The course recognizes the importance of applying social justice approaches and cultural humility to various aspects of practice on all levels including assessment, planning, research, intervention, and evaluation.	{"code": "SOWK 4368", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3319	course	SOWK 4369	SOWK 4369 Adolescent Development: Challenges and Safety Adolescence is a critical period marked by physical, cognitive, emotional, and social transformations. This course explores the unique developmental processes, challenges, and safety considerations associated with adolescence. It aims to equip students with a comprehensive understanding of the factors influencing adolescent development and the strategies to address the challenges they may encounter.	{"code": "SOWK 4369", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3320	course	SOWK 4370	SOWK 4370 Social Determinants of Health Introduces students to the population health perspective, social determinants of health, health disparities, and health equity. Social workers need to know how to identify and address social determinants of health and health disparities and ways social work policy and practice can improve health equity for affected populations so that the community will be more responsive and effective to community needs with clients. This course facilitates the development of advanced practice skills in engaging and assessing organizations.	{"code": "SOWK 4370", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3321	course	SOWK 4371	SOWK 4371 Military Policy, Intervention, and Treatment This specialized populations course focuses on the study of military policy, intervention, and treatment from a social work perspective. Students will explore the historical and contemporary policies affecting military personnel, with an emphasis on mental health, trauma-informed care, and family support systems. The course includes an in-depth analysis of intervention strategies and treatment modalities, supported by current research and best practices.	{"code": "SOWK 4371", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3322	course	SOWK 4372	SOWK 4372 Sports Social Work Provides students with a wide-ranging review of sport social work. This course will help social workers interested in the field learn how to effectively promote the safety and well-being of athletes, advocate for athlete rights, and ensure athletes receive the recognition and help needed to become strong global leaders. This course will discuss (1) interprofessional collaboration with others in the athletic space, (2) health and well-being issues facing athletes, (3) sport social work theory, (4) engaging and assessing athlete needs, (5) clinical interventions in sport, and (6) an introduction to sport social work policy.	{"code": "SOWK 4372", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
