3625	course	THEA 3256	THEA 3256 Drawing and Rendering for the Theatre Foundational drawing and rendering concepts developed for students of the theatre. Through practice and disciplined application, students master drawing and rendering skills in order to effectively communicate aesthetic choices for application in theatre design.	{"code": "THEA 3256", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3626	course	THEA 3290	THEA 3290 Acting on Camera A discussion of foundational concepts regarding screen acting technique and strategically applying this technique to create on-camera performances. Students work in various genres with the intentions of mastering the foundations of objective-driven, realistic acting. Students also analyze a film, critiquing the acting using the language of craft discussed in the class. Four contact hours.	{"code": "THEA 3290", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3668	course	THEA 4800	THEA 4800 Directed Independent Study Allows students to pursue faculty-directed independent study topics (1) of special interest to the student, (2) within the area of the instructor’s special competence, (3) not provided by other Department offerings.	{"code": "THEA 4800", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3627	course	THEA 3293	THEA 3293 Introduction to African American Theatre A discussion-centered course designed to give students of all backgrounds an introductory understanding to the world of modern African American theatre. Covers elements of style, design, criticism, and history as they pertain to the genre through reading, discussion, and performance. Students explore, discuss, and analyze issues of politics, race, and gender in the American theatre. Emphasis is placed on major playwrights, theatre companies, and performers within their historical and social contexts.	{"code": "THEA 3293", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
3628	course	THEA 3300	THEA 3300 Directing I An introductory course in the fundamentals of directing that focuses on defining the role of the director while discovering a variety of directorial strategies. Processes explored include text analysis, collaboration and leadership, director/actor communication, directing theory, storytelling (narrative), point of view, and stage composition. Students practice applying the concepts and skills they have learned in the class by directing short scenes, acting in short scenes, and participating in an analysis of student work.	{"code": "THEA 3300", "catalog": "Undergraduate 2026-2027"}	\N
